{"name":"Library Pewter","hex":"#7f7263","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"library","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An institution which holds books and/or other forms of media for use by the public or qualified people often lending them out, as well as providing various other services for its users.","Any institution that lends out its goods for use by the public or a community."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/library"},{"term":"pewter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An alloy of approximately 93–98% tin and 1–2% copper, and the balance of antimony.","An alloy of tin and lead."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark, dull grey colour, like that of the metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pewter"}],"entries":[{"title":"Library","blocks":[{"text":"A library is a collection of books, and possibly other materials and media, that is accessible for use by its members and members of allied institutions. Libraries provide physical or digital materials, and may be a physical location, a virtual space, or both. A library's collection normally includes printed materials which can be borrowed, and usually also includes a reference section of publications which may only be utilized inside the premises. Resources such as commercial releases of films, television programmes, other video recordings, radio, music and audio recordings may be available in many formats. These include DVDs, Blu-rays, CDs, cassettes, or other applicable formats such as microform. They may also provide access to information, music or other content held on bibliographic databases. In addition, some libraries offer creation stations for makers which offer access to a 3D printing station with a 3D scanner."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/SanDiegoCityCollegeLearningResource_-_bookshelf.jpg/330px-SanDiegoCityCollegeLearningResource_-_bookshelf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library","wikidata":"Q7075"},{"title":"Pewter","blocks":[{"text":"Pewter is a malleable metal alloy consisting of tin (85–99%), antimony, copper (2%), bismuth, and sometimes silver. In the past, it was an alloy of tin and as much as 40% lead, but most modern pewter, in order to prevent lead poisoning, is not made with lead. Pewter has a low melting point, around 170–230 °C (338–446 °F), depending on the exact mixture of metals. The word pewter is possibly a variation of \"spelter\", a term for zinc alloys."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pewter","wikidata":"Q852372"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Library_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/500px-Library_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Library_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/1280px-Library_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Library_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg/500px-Library_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Library of Ashurbanipal in Mesopotamia, c. 1500–539 BC.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALibrary_of_Ashurbanipal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Videos_in_boxes_in_Monmouth_County_Library_eastern_branch.JPG/500px-Videos_in_boxes_in_Monmouth_County_Library_eastern_branch.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Videos_in_boxes_in_Monmouth_County_Library_eastern_branch.JPG/500px-Videos_in_boxes_in_Monmouth_County_Library_eastern_branch.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVid